\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Table of best fits to \citet {10.1093/mnras/stv2900}~model of the spatial distribution (see Eq. \ref {eq_han_evolved}). We include fits spanning the range of halo masses and redshifts included in our suite of {\tt galacticus}~models, as well as at the redshift and host halo mass, $M_h = 10^{13} \mathrm {M}_\odot $ and redshift, $z = 0.5$ where $\Sigma _{\mathrm {sub}}$ is defined. For comparison, we include fits to Aquarius and Phoenix halos provided by \citet {10.1093/mnras/stv2900}~. }}{19}{table.struct.897}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces Table of best fits for the projected subhalo mass function (PSHMF, Eq. \ref {eq_massfunction_proj} and \ref {eq_massfunction_proj_evolved}). Fitting coefficients are averaged over 224 host halos per redshift and host halo mass (22,400 host halos in total). Projections are taken within a $10-20$ kpc annulus, and averaged over 3 projections in the xy, yz, and xz planes. Due to the low number of halos in the aperture, halo to halo scatter for the SHMF slope cannot be calculated. }}{28}{table.struct.1158}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Amplitude of the evolved PSHMF, given in terms of the $f_{\mathrm {s}} \cdot \Sigma _{\mathrm {sub}}$ (see Eq. \ref {eq_massfunction_proj_evolved}). In the ``scaled'' column, the number density has been extrapolated to a $10^{13} \mathrm {M}_{\odot }$ halo at redshift $z=0.5$ using the scaling relations presented in this work. A logarithmic slope of the subhalo mass function $\alpha = -1.97$ was assumed. }}{28}{table.struct.1219}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2.4}{\ignorespaces The best fits to power law scaling relations predicted by our {\tt galacticus}~models and estimates using the \citet {10.1093/mnras/stv2900}~model. The upper section gives the scaling for the unevolved case, while the lower section gives the scaling results for the evolved case. We compare the scaling relation for the unevolved case for the scaling relation which would be obtained if the scaling was entirely determined by the host's density profile evolution (Eq. \ref {eq_han_unevolved}). For the evolved case, we compare the PSHMF scaling prediction to that of the \citet {10.1093/mnras/stv2900}~model (Eq. \ref {eq_han_evolved}). The transfer function from the host's smooth dark matter density profile is parameterized as a power law, with logarithmic slope $\gamma $ (Eq. \ref {eq_han_transfer}). We consider two possibilities for $\gamma $, one where $\gamma $ is a constant and the other with $\gamma $ a function of halo mass. For the case of mass independent $\gamma $, we compute the scaling coefficients for a range of physically meaningful values of $\gamma $. Finally, to study the effect of $\gamma $ varying systematically with mass, we linearly interpolate the best-fit $\gamma $ values as a function of mass (Table \ref {tab_han_fits}) to compute the scaling relations (``$\gamma $ interp''). }}{29}{table.struct.1303}%
